1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,300
Your teacher starts a sentence.

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:04,260
You finish it in your head, twice.

3
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:09,200
A pop quiz lands on your desk, 10 problems, done before the

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:10,180
paper stops sliding.

5
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:13,340
Across the room, a pencil rolls off a desk.

6
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:15,460
You already know where it will land.

7
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:19,900
A glance at the clock, the second hand hasn't moved, so you

8
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:20,560
plan your weekend.

9
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:24,840
Name the fly on the window, solve tomorrow's homework, and

10
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:26,700
the pencil still rolling.

11
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:31,440
A normal conversation runs at about 150 words per minute.

12
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:33,740
Your brain could follow 1500,

13
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:35,540
10 times faster.

14
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:37,460
Sounds perfect, right?

15
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:38,700
Here's the catch.

16
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:44,080
Your brain sped up, your eyes didn't, your legs didn't, your

17
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:48,040
nerves still carry signals at the same old speed, up to about

18
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:53,120
120 meters per second, and the world, it runs at one speed.

19
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:55,180
Thinking faster is easy.

20
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:57,220
Making anything else faster?

21
00:00:57,820 --> 00:00:58,140
Impossible.

22
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:02,060
So what if your brain processed information 10 times faster?

23
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:03,180
Monday,

24
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:05,520
the exam lasts 90 minutes.

25
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:09,880
You need nine, five answer checks, then the ceiling tiles,

26
00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:10,780
212.

27
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:12,700
Done already?

28
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:14,880
Your teacher asks, check again.

29
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:16,420
You did, twice.

30
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:19,460
At lunch, your friend Mia tells a story.

31
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:20,960
It takes four minutes.

32
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:23,120
For you, it takes 40.

33
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:24,440
You nod politely.

34
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:27,200
Inside, you reorganize your locker.

35
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:31,020
In baseball class, a fastball crosses the plate in about

36
00:01:31,020 --> 00:01:32,300
four-tenths of a second.

37
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:34,860
To you, that feels like four seconds.

38
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:38,400
You see the pitch, plan the swing, replan it.

39
00:01:38,820 --> 00:01:40,820
Your arm still needs its normal time.

40
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:42,500
Strike one.

41
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:43,320
Seriously?

42
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:46,600
In chess club, you get three minutes on the clock.

43
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:48,160
It feels like 30.

44
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:53,400
You beat the school champion in 11 moves, plus you win, minus.

45
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:55,140
Nobody wants to play you twice.

46
00:01:55,940 --> 00:01:57,860
On Friday, you try an online game.

47
00:01:58,400 --> 00:01:59,740
Your brain decides in a flash.

48
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:02,500
Then the signal crawls to the server.

49
00:02:03,060 --> 00:02:03,700
40 milliseconds.

50
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:05,760
Lag beats genius.

51
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:07,640
Everyone blames the internet.

52
00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:09,100
You blame physics.

53
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:13,160
Then Dr. Lee, a neuroscientist, scans your head.

54
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,780
Her screen glows like a city at night.

55
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:17,160
She flashes numbers.

56
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:20,480
Most people hold about four things in mind at once.

57
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:23,980
You hold four, faster, not bigger.

58
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:27,900
Your brain already burns about 20% of your body's energy,

59
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:28,560
she says.

60
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:30,380
Fast thinking isn't free.

61
00:02:30,940 --> 00:02:34,000
Eat, sleep, and never sign anything in a hurry.

62
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:37,960
Right on cue, a man in a gray coat waits at the school gate.

63
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:42,540
Mr. Black, private institute, full scholarship, huge salary.

64
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:43,640
One condition,

65
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:45,900
signed by Sunday at five.

66
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:48,020
Who sets a deadline like that?

67
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:49,440
But there's a bill.

68
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:51,360
By evening, you're starving.

69
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:54,020
Your head pounds, like a drum.

70
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:56,500
A faster brain burns more fuel.

71
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:58,320
And yours runs hot.

72
00:02:58,940 --> 00:02:59,440
Worth it?

73
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:00,280
Worse,

74
00:03:00,980 --> 00:03:01,840
life gets flat.

75
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:05,380
Part of your reward system responds most to surprises.

76
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:08,040
You predict nearly everything now.

77
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:09,160
Fewer surprises,

78
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:10,880
less sparkle.

79
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:12,320
Pretending, too.

80
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:14,580
A fake frown on quizzes.

81
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:17,140
Fake surprise at jokes you saw coming.

82
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:18,160
And waiting?

83
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:20,640
Every minute feels like 10.

84
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:24,200
Every conversation feels like a bus that never comes.

85
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,800
Who do you talk to when everyone moves in slow motion?

86
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:29,940
Only one person tries.

87
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:31,040
Mia.

88
00:03:31,860 --> 00:03:33,420
She walks home with you every day.

89
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:35,180
Always half a step behind.

90
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:36,780
You rush ahead.

91
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:38,660
She taps your shoulder twice.

92
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:40,160
Where's the fire?

93
00:03:40,820 --> 00:03:42,220
Wait for me, she says.

94
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:43,240
You stop.

95
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:45,580
One day, you ask why she bothers.

96
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:47,080
She shrugs.

97
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:49,080
Fast is great, she says.

98
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:51,100
Just don't leave anyone behind.

99
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:52,660
Then she grins.

100
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:55,720
Besides, somebody has to laugh at your jokes.

101
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:57,320
Even the third time.

102
00:03:58,000 --> 00:03:58,440
Two taps,

103
00:03:59,160 --> 00:03:59,580
you wait.

104
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:02,780
Sunday, 5 o'clock, the park.

105
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:05,840
Mr. Black slides a contract across a bench.

106
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:07,800
Sign here, he says.

107
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:09,380
Your pen hovers.

108
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:13,480
Across the grass, Mia's little brother Sam climbs the playground

109
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:13,900
frame.

110
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:15,300
He waves at you.

111
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:16,800
Four meters up.

112
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:18,060
His foot slips.

113
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:19,540
He doesn't jump.

114
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:20,940
He just drops.

115
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:22,580
Time stretches.

116
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:25,560
Ten seconds of thought inside one.

117
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:28,020
Mia is halfway across the park.

118
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:28,900
Mouth open.

119
00:04:29,660 --> 00:04:30,420
A pigeon hangs.

120
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:31,560
Mid-flap.

121
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:33,540
Nobody else has moved yet.

122
00:04:34,220 --> 00:04:34,980
You see his hands.

123
00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:35,900
The rail.

124
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:36,900
The ground.

125
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:41,100
A drop from four meters lasts about nine tenths of a second.

126
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:42,900
You're six meters away.

127
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:46,600
A fit teenager sprints about seven meters per second.

128
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:48,620
That's eight and a half tenths.

129
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:50,000
Almost no margin.

130
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:53,460
A normal brain needs a quarter of a second just to start

131
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:53,720
moving.

132
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:55,700
You saw the wobble coming.

133
00:04:56,260 --> 00:04:56,920
You already started.

134
00:04:57,700 --> 00:04:59,320
Your legs can't run any faster.

135
00:05:00,060 --> 00:05:02,120
Your brain can only make you start sooner.

136
00:05:03,020 --> 00:05:04,240
Pen or Sam.

137
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:06,260
Don't leave anyone behind.

138
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:07,780
You drop the pen.

139
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:08,740
You run.

140
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:09,860
One meter.

141
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:10,720
Two.

142
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:12,680
Air hums in your ears.

143
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:13,780
Four.

144
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:15,720
His sneaker leaves the rail.

145
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:16,720
Five.

146
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:17,680
You dive.

147
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:18,900
Not a catch.

148
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:19,880
A landing.

149
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:21,060
Wump.

150
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:22,320
You hit the grass.

151
00:05:23,060 --> 00:05:24,420
Sam lands on top of you.

152
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:25,320
He blinks.

153
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:27,100
Then he laughs.

154
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:28,460
Bruised ribs.

155
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:29,580
Nothing worse.

156
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:31,360
Mia sprints up.

157
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:32,060
Out of breath.

158
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:33,340
Wait for me.

159
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:34,380
She gasps.

160
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:36,080
Two taps on your shoulder.

161
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:38,680
For once, you're already waiting.

162
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:41,220
Mr. Black strolls over, smiling.

163
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:42,840
Extraordinary.

164
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:44,920
Sign and I'll make you famous.

165
00:05:45,820 --> 00:05:46,720
You pick up the contract.

166
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:50,540
Your brain reads all nine pages before his smile fades.

167
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:51,840
Page nine.

168
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:53,740
No contact with family.

169
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:54,800
Twelve months.

170
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:56,320
Would you sign that?

171
00:05:56,760 --> 00:05:56,980
You ask.

172
00:05:57,680 --> 00:05:58,820
Parents nearby stare.

173
00:05:59,580 --> 00:05:59,820
At him.

174
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:01,480
He has no answer.

175
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:04,300
A good offer survives a good night's sleep.

176
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:06,920
Rushing you to sign is a red flag.

177
00:06:07,660 --> 00:06:08,880
No thanks, you say.

178
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:10,660
I don't leave anyone behind.

179
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:15,260
For the first time in months, a minute feels like exactly

180
00:06:15,260 --> 00:06:16,280
one minute.

181
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:18,720
That night, your phone buzzes.

182
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:20,080
Unknown number.

183
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:21,380
Four words.

184
00:06:22,180 --> 00:06:23,520
Ten times is nothing.

185
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:25,340
Who sends that?

186
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:27,720
And what comes after ten?

187
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:31,240
So what would you do with a brain ten times faster?

188
00:06:32,050 --> 00:06:35,140
Tell us in the comments what you'd do with ten hours squeezed

189
00:06:35,140 --> 00:06:35,780
into one.

190
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:40,120
And if you'd wait for a friend, like and subscribe for more

191
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:41,060
What If episodes.
